consumer_reviews.statement:
Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ($\mathrm{AC}_\omega$), inherited from the Lebesgue interface ([[def-countable-choice]]). Let $a>0$ and let $\rho\in L^1(\mathbb R^3)$ be real and vanish $\lambda_3$-a.e. outside $\overline B_a(0)$; let $E_{\mathrm{ext}}:\mathbb R^3\to\mathbb R^3$ be a prescribed external electric field ([[def-em-fields]]) that is continuous on $\overline B_a(0)$ and Lipschitz there with constant $L\ge0$, i.e. $|E_{\mathrm{ext}}(x)-E_{\mathrm{ext}}(x')|\le L|x-x'|$ for all $x,x'\in\overline B_a(0)$. Consider the matter model in which the prescribed static distribution $\rho$ is carried by a body at rest in the laboratory frame and the only electromagnetic force taken into account is the external-field part of the continuum coupling of [[post-em-lorentz-coupling]] (ii), namely the force density $\rho E_{\mathrm{ext}}$; the self-field of $\rho$, all other fields, mechanical support and back-reaction are excluded by this model. Define the **total external force** and the **total external torque about the origin** by the componentwise Lebesgue integrals
$$F:=\int_{\mathbb R^3}\rho(y)\,E_{\mathrm{ext}}(y)\,d\lambda_3(y)\in\mathbb R^3,\qquad \tau:=\int_{\mathbb R^3}\rho(y)\,\bigl(y\times E_{\mathrm{ext}}(y)\bigr)\,d\lambda_3(y)\in\mathbb R^3,$$
absolutely convergent because on the support $|E_{\mathrm{ext}}|\le|E_{\mathrm{ext}}(0)|+La$ and $|y\times E_{\mathrm{ext}}|\le a(|E_{\mathrm{ext}}(0)|+La)$, so each component is dominated by a constant times $|\rho|\in L^1$ ([[def-integral-over-a-measurable-set]], [[def-integrable-real-and-complex-functions-and-their-integrals]], [[def-cross-product-in-r3]], [[thm-cross-product-norm-is-the-two-vector-gram-determinant]], [[thm-integral-triangle-inequality]], [[thm-monotonicity-of-the-integral]]). Then:
(a) [Uniform force and torque expansions]
$$F=Q\,E_{\mathrm{ext}}(0)+R_F,\qquad |R_F|\le L\,a\,\|\rho\|_1,\qquad \tau=p\times E_{\mathrm{ext}}(0)+R_\tau,\qquad |R_\tau|\le L\,a^2\|\rho\|_1,$$
where $Q$ and $p$ are the total charge and dipole moment of [[def-em-electric-moments]] (a) and $\|\rho\|_1=\int|\rho|\,d\lambda_3$;
(b) [Neutral distribution] if $Q=0$ then the leading force term vanishes, $F=R_F$ with $|R_F|\le La\|\rho\|_1$, and the torque is the ideal-dipole torque $p\times E_{\mathrm{ext}}(0)$ up to the bound $|R_\tau|\le La^2\|\rho\|_1$; in this sense a neutral distribution of size $a$ in a field with Lipschitz constant $L$ carries the dipole torque $p\times E_{\mathrm{ext}}(0)$ with an explicit remainder;
(c) [Reference-point shift] for any reference point $o\in\mathbb R^3$, the torque about $o$, $\tau_o:=\int_{\mathbb R^3}\rho(y)\,(y-o)\times E_{\mathrm{ext}}(y)\,d\lambda_3(y)$, equals $\tau-o\times F$ exactly;
(d) [Ideal pair of test charges at rest] let $q\in\mathbb R$ and $d\in\mathbb R^3\setminus\{0\}$, put $a=|d|/2$, and consider the two stationary point charges $+q$ at $d/2$ and $-q$ at $-d/2$ ([[def-em-electric-moments]] (c)), each treated as a test charge at rest in the laboratory chart in the same prescribed external field $E_{\mathrm{ext}}$, with the mutual interaction and the self-field of each charge excluded by the model. Then the spatial part of the adopted equation of motion of [[post-em-lorentz-coupling]] (i) at zero coordinate velocity is $m\,dU^i/d\tau=q'E^i_{\mathrm{ext}}(x)$ for a charge $q'$ at $x$, because at rest $U=(c,0,0,0)$ and $U_0=-c$ ([[def-em-relativistic-worldlines-frames]] (b),(c)) while $F^{i0}=-E_i/c$ ([[def-em-electromagnetic-tensor]] (a)); consequently the total force and the total torque about the origin of the pair satisfy the expansions of (a) and (b) with $Q=0$, $p=qd$ and $|q|+|{-q}|=2|q|$ in place of $\|\rho\|_1$: $F=R_F$ with $|R_F|\le2|q|La$ and $\tau=p\times E_{\mathrm{ext}}(0)+R_\tau$ with $|R_\tau|\le2|q|La^2$;
(e) [Scope] the statement is conditional on the explicitly stated matter model: only the prescribed external field acts on the distribution, the distribution is prescribed and at rest, and no self-force, back-reaction, mechanical equilibrium, stability or dynamics of the body is asserted; the external field is a datum, not solved for. Clause (d) is an instantaneous idealisation of two ideal point test charges with mutual interaction excluded; it is not a two-body dynamics, a bound-state or a stability statement. No radiation, medium response or empirical claim is made.

Let $\Omega\subseteq\mathbb R^3$ be open, star-shaped ([[def-star-shaped-open-subset-of-rn]]) and the spatial region of a pure electrostatic regime as in [[pthm-em-electrostatic-potential]], with fields of [[def-em-fields]], charge parameters of [[def-em-sources]] and the units of [[def-em-si-conventions]]; thus $E:\Omega\to\mathbb R^3$ is $C^1$, $B=0$ and $J=0$ ([[def-em-electrostatic-regime]]), and by [[pthm-em-electrostatic-potential]] there is $\varphi\in C^2(\Omega)$ with $E=-\nabla\varphi$. Let $q\in\mathbb R$ (coulombs) be the charge of a test particle and let $\gamma:[t_0,t_1]\to\Omega$ be a piecewise $C^1$ path ([[def-interval]], [[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]]). Define the **work of the electric force** along the prescribed path by the vector line integral
$$W(\gamma):=\int_\gamma qE\cdot d\mathbf r$$
of [[def-scalar-and-vector-line-integrals-along-piecewise-c1-paths]]; $qE$ is the electric part of the external force adopted for a test charge in [[post-em-lorentz-coupling]](i). Then:
(a) [Value] $W(\gamma)=q\,(\varphi(\gamma(t_0))-\varphi(\gamma(t_1)))$;
(b) [Path independence] if $\gamma_1,\gamma_2$ are piecewise $C^1$ paths in $\Omega$ with the same initial and the same terminal point, then $W(\gamma_1)=W(\gamma_2)$; in particular $W(\gamma)=0$ for every closed piecewise $C^1$ path;
(c) [Potential energy] with the potential energy $U:=q\varphi$ (joules) the identity reads $W(\gamma)=-(U(\gamma(t_1))-U(\gamma(t_0)))$;
(d) [Gauge invariance of the work] if $\varphi' \in C^2(\Omega)$ is any other potential with $\nabla\varphi'=-E$, then $\varphi'=\varphi+c$ with a constant $c\in\mathbb R$ and the formula of (a) is unchanged ([[cor-potentials-differ-by-a-componentwise-constant]]).
The statement is a line-integral identity for a prescribed path in a prescribed static external field. It asserts no equation of motion, no radiation, no work done on the sources and no claim that the test particle actually follows $\gamma$; the interpretation of $W$ as work done by the adopted external coupling is the physical reading supplied by [[post-em-lorentz-coupling]], not a dynamical theorem.
